          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In much of mathematics, certainly in the traditional treatment of mathematical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+in+model+theory"} structures
            from the point of view of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+theory"} model theory
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/embedding"} embedding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
            between structures that preserves
            %em and reflects
            the structure specified within a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/language"} language
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %p
            But for categories themselves, the situation and practice is more complicated, with no notion of “embedding of categories” that is universally accepted in the literature. A critical dividing line, leading to several distinct notions of embedding, lies in how one views the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} category of categories
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                If we view categories as mathematical structures in the traditional sense, with central concepts being definable in the ordinary 1-category of categories and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
                (which are the relevant structure-preserving maps), then notions of embedding tend to emphasize they should be
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+maps"} injective maps
                on the classes of objects (and more besides).
            %li
              %p
                If we view categories however as having a higher-dimensional aspect, i.e., if we view central concepts as being based rather on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
                of categories, functors,
                %em and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
                (and is therefore rejected).
          %p In this article we survey the various notions of categorical embedding that have appeared in the literature, and try to describe some of the underlying contexts and rationales.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#1categorical_definitions 1-categorical definitions
          %p Perhaps the most obvious 1-categorical definition of embedding is:
          %ul
            %li
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: C \to D
              that is both injective on objects and a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful functor
          %p
            This definition is adopted in
            %a{:href => "#AHS"} Adamek-Herrlich-Strecker
            and in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Riehl"} Riehl
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+categories"} strict categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphisms"} monomorphisms
            in the
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
          %p
            If we define categories with
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "/nlab/show/category#OneCollectionOfMorphisms"} one collection of morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            on morphisms is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+function"} injective function
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Mor
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Mor
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mor(C) \to Mor(D)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(c) = F(c')
            for objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c, c'
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(1_c) = F(1_{c'})
            and thus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi c
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_c = 1_{c'}
            by injectivity on morphisms, whence
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo =
                    %mi c
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c = c'
          %p
            However, as noted above, in many cases an “embedding” means something
            %em stronger
            than a mere monomorphism, being required to reflect “all the structure”. For instance, an embedding of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            is a subspace inclusion, not merely an injective continuous map (the latter being the monos in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
          %p In particular, the above definition of embedding does not even reflect the property of objects being isomorphic, so that it is not necessarily injective on isomorphism classes of objects! This suggests that a (still 1-categorical) embedding of categories ought to be something stronger. Some possibilities include:
          %ul
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+monomorphisms"} regular monomorphisms
              or
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extremal+monomorphisms"} extremal monomorphisms
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
              (what are those?)
            %li
              Monomorphisms in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              that are also
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+functors"} full functors
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functors"} fully faithful functors
              = succeed "." do
                %strong full embeddings
            %li
              Monomorphisms in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              that are also injective on isomorphism classes of objects (this is used in
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toposes%2C+Triples%2C+and+Theories"} Toposes, Triples, and Theories
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudomonic+functors"} pseudomonic functors
          %h3#2categorical_definitions 2-categorical definitions
          %p Most of the above 1-categorical notions of embedding corresponds to a “pure” 2-categorical notion by simply removing the injectivity on objects. Thus we have the following candidates:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functors"} faithful functors
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functors"} fully faithful functors
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudomonic+functors"} pseudomonic functors
          %p
            The choice between these is closely related to the question of giving an equivalence-invariant notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
          %h3#relationship Relationship
          %p
            Every fully faithful functor is
            %em equivalent
            to one that is fully faithful and injective on objects. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:C\to D
            be fully faithful, and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            be the “mapping cylinder” category, whose objects are the disjoint unions of those of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            embedded fully-faithfully (and of course injectively on objects), and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(c,d) = D(f c,d)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(d,c) = D(d,f c)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            allow us to compose all arrows in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            making it a category. There is a projection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E\to D
            that is the identity on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            and acts by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D\to E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:C\to D
            is equivalent, in the 2-category whose objects are functors and whose morphisms are pseudo-commutative squares, to the inclusion functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to E
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              Full embeddings are closed under
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushouts"} pushouts
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
              %ul
                %li
                  For ordinary categories this was proven first by
                  %a{:href => "#Trnková"} Trnková
                  under the assumption both functors are full embeddings, and later in the general case by
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "#FL"} Fritch and Latch
                %li
                  For
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+categories"} enriched categories
                  it is proven independently in
                  %a{:href => "#BKPV"} BKPV13, Lemma 4.9
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "#Stanculescu"} Stanculescu, Prop. 3.1
                %li
                  For
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
                  it is proven in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "#Simpson"} Simspon, Cor. 16.6.2
                  %a{:href => "#MS"} MacDonald and Scull
                  for more general conditions.
                %li
                  For
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories"} internal categories
                  in an
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "#MW"} Martini and Wolf
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#the_yoneda_embedding The Yoneda embedding
          %p
            Probably the most common embedding of categories encountered is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "/nlab/show/category#AFamilyOfCollectionsOfMorphisms"} a family of collections of morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(z,x) = C(z,y)
            as sets for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z\in C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            to literally the same presheaf. (Such an equality of hom-sets is not as weird as it might sound; for instance, when regarding a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preordered+set"} preordered set
            as a category it’s natural to define every nonempty homset to be the
            %em same
            = succeed ".)" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                terminal set
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/terminal+set"} ?
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barr+embedding+theorem"} Barr embedding theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/embedding"} embedding
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
          %div
            %p
              %strong basic properties of…
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
                %ul
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful functor
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+functor"} full functor
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful functor
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective-on-objects+functor"} injective-on-objects functor
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+injective+functor"} essentially injective functor
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/embedding+of+categories"} embedding of categories
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective functor
                    %ul
                      %li
                        %p
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjective-on-objects+functor"} surjective-on-objects functor
                      %li
                        %p
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijective-on-objects+functor"} bijective-on-objects functor
                      %li
                        %p
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dominant+functor"} dominant functor
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+essentially+surjective+functor"} split essentially surjective functor
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+and+full+functor"} essentially surjective and full functor
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conservative+functor"} conservative functor
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization+functor"} localization functor
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudomonic+functor"} pseudomonic functor
                  %li
                    %p
                      (
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocontinuous+functor"}> co
                      )
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functor"} continuous functor
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/final+functor"}> final functor
                      ,
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+functor"} dense functor
              %li
                %p
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-functors"} 2-functors
                %ul
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+2-functor"} full and faithful 2-functor
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+fully+faithful+2-functor"} locally fully faithful 2-functor
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+2-categories"} equivalence of 2-categories
              %li
                %p
                  %strong>
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"} (∞,1)-functors
                  \:
                %ul
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} fully faithful (∞,1)-functor
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} essentially surjective (∞,1)-functor
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} equivalence of (∞,1)-categories
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+final+functor"}> homotopy final functor
                      ,
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/final+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} final (∞,1)-functor
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#AHS
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ji%C5%99%C3%AD+Ad%C3%A1mek"}> Jiří Adámek
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Horst+Herrlich"} Horst Herrlich
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Strecker"} George Strecker
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Abstract and Concrete Categories: The Joy of Cats
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://katmat.math.uni-bremen.de/acc/"} Online edition
            %li#Riehl
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"}> Emily Riehl
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Category Theory in Context
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://emilyriehl.github.io/files/context.pdf"} pdf
          %p For stability under pushouts:
          %ul
            %li{:id => "Trnková"}
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/V%C4%9Bra+Trnkov%C3%A1"}> Věra Trnková
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Sum of categories with amalgamated subcategory
            %li#FL
              %p
                R. Fritsch, D. M. Latch,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopy inverses for nerve
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01214196"} 10.1007/BF01214196
            %li#MS
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+MacDonald"} John MacDonald
                and Laura Scull,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Amalgamations of categories
            %li#BKPV
              %p
                Bilkova, M., Kurz, A., Petrisan, D., &amp; Velebil, J. (2013),
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Relation lifting, with an application to the many-valued cover modality
            %li#Stanculescu
              %p
                Alexandru E. Stanculescu,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Constructing model categories with prescribed fibrant objects
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/29/23/29-23abs.html"} journal page
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.6005"} 1208.6005
            %li#Simpson
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"}> Carlos Simpson
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+Theory+of+Higher+Categories"} Homotopy Theory of Higher Categories
            %li#MW
              %p
                Louis Martini and Sebastian Wolf,
                %em Colimits and cocompletions in internal higher category theory
                (2024),
                = succeed "," do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Higher Structures
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Higher+Structures"} ?
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://higher-structures.math.cas.cz/api/files/issues/Vol8Iss1/MartiniWolf"} pdf
